While monitoring activity in my home SOC lab, one of my honeypots triggered an alert for suspicious SSH authentication activity.

The alert fired after detecting more than 20 failed login attempts within a five-minute window targeting the root account.

Initial Details

Host: MTS-Honeypot
Source IP: 187[.]191[.]2[.]213
Event: Failed SSH Authentication
Time: 2026-03-02 08:40 UTC

Investigation

Reviewing the logs showed repeated password authentication attempts generated by sshd. The attacker consistently targeted the root account, along with several invalid usernames, which is a common pattern in automated credential scanning.

Key findings:

Multiple failed SSH authentication attempts

Repeated targeting of privileged accounts

No successful login events

No suspicious processes or persistence activity

No lateral movement indicators

Because this system is a honeypot intentionally exposed to the internet, the activity aligns with automated SSH brute force scanning performed by bots searching for weak credentials.

Conclusion

This alert was a true positive detection of malicious brute force activity, but there was no compromise since authentication never succeeded.

Security takeaway

If SSH is exposed to the internet, it will be scanned. Often within minutes.

Basic hardening steps make a big difference:

Disable root SSH login
Use SSH key-based authentication
Implement rate limiting or fail2ban
Restrict SSH access to trusted IP ranges or VPN

Honeypots continue to be a powerful way to observe attacker behavior and practice real-world investigations.
